  "note": "An intermediate guitar plus theory and ear week at 30 minutes a day: 7th chords and the ii-V-I are heard, named, played as shapes, arpeggios and guide tones, moved to a new key and improvised over, with an ear or theory exercise every day between the guitar work. Each step says what to press and where to look.",
  "plan": {
    "title": "Week 1: 7th chords and the ii-V-I",
    "summary": "Hear and name 7th chords, then play the ii-V-I as shapes, arpeggios, guide tones and solos in C and G, with the relative minor and a blues for contrast.",
    "progress": "Techniques: alternate picking at 90, string skipping at 80, legato trills. Steady; used as warm-ups.\nExtended chords: open ii-V-I shapes in C and G, comping with the backing track at 80, a 12-bar in A at 90. Next: the same shapes higher up the neck.\nCAGED: arpeggios of Dm7, G7 and Cmaj7 from memory, guide tones through the changes. Next: the arpeggios in G without the chart.\nScales: improvising over ii-V-I landing on 3rds and 7ths; A minor pentatonic with the major 3rd over the blues. Next: longer phrases across two chords.\nBasic chords: heard and played C-G-Am-F against Am-F-C-G. Solid.\nBuilding chords: names major, minor, 7, maj7 and m7 by ear, faster by day 5. Next: add diminished.\nProgressions & styles: finds ii, V and I in C, hears I-vi-ii-V, knows why the blues uses three 7th chords. Next: ii-V-I in a minor key.\nScales & keys: knows C major and A minor share their notes. Next: the same in G and E minor.",
